rance of the solemn word”\",\"supports\":\"Shows that this is his first explicit declaration and that it is emotionally weighty; choking indicates intense delivery but does not independently establish E.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“and your rules shall be mine. Give me a chance of winning you.”\",\"supports\":\"Places the declaration within a serious courtship undertaking and supplies moderate support for P, while also showing that much of the explicit commitment lies in adjacent language.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Monica insists that she “must still be perfectly free,” and Widdowson answers, “Perfectly!”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the declaration occurs amid negotiation of relational terms rather than constituting an accepted engagement or reciprocal commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the direct avowal, while P captures its limited undertaking force in courtship. Emotional delivery can be recorded contextually without E, and no important aspect of the core force falls outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
